WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.529
With Chappie out soon, let’s take a look
at 8 things you probably didn’t know about

00:00:04.529 --> 00:00:11.980
director Neill Blomkamp’s latest sci-fi
movie starring Hugh Jackman and Sharlto Copley.

00:00:11.980 --> 00:00:17.500
During filming, actor Sharlto Copley, who
plays former police droid Chappie, wore a

00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:20.040
skin-tight, grey suit with tracking markers
on it.

00:00:20.040 --> 00:00:25.109
The visual effects team painted the robot
over Copley’s performance, using the actor’s

00:00:25.109 --> 00:00:26.769
movements to bring Chappie to life.

00:00:26.769 --> 00:00:31.640
Over his grey suit, Copley wore two other
pieces of costume to help his performance.

00:00:31.640 --> 00:00:37.410
First, he wore a chest plate, which kept his
back and chest proportions the same as Chappie’s,

00:00:37.410 --> 00:00:42.390
and helped him know what spaces Chappie could
fit in and how he should sit or stand in certain

00:00:42.390 --> 00:00:43.390
positions.

00:00:43.390 --> 00:00:48.470
And, when, for example, an actor grabbed Chappie’s
shoulder, the chest plate made sure the actor’s

00:00:48.470 --> 00:00:52.820
hands were in the right place when Copley
was later digitally replaced by Chappie.

00:00:52.820 --> 00:00:57.720
Secondly, over his grey lycra suit, Copley
wore a pair of shorts and a belt, which he

00:00:57.720 --> 00:01:02.160
loosened and dropped half way down his backside
to create the right kind of moves to bring

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:05.990
gangster Chappie to life!

00:01:05.990 --> 00:01:09.420
The movie Chappie is based on a short film
about robot police that writer-director Neill

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:12.070
Blomkamp made back in 2003.

00:01:12.070 --> 00:01:15.510
That short film was called Tetra Vaal.

00:01:15.510 --> 00:01:20.040
The design of the robot Chappie was influenced
by director Neill Blomkamp’s love of manga

00:01:20.040 --> 00:01:21.070
and anime.

00:01:21.070 --> 00:01:26.490
Specifically, he was inspired by Japanese
manga artist Masamune Shirow and his rabbit-eared

00:01:26.490 --> 00:01:33.390
robot called Briareos, which features in the
1980s manga series Appleseed.

00:01:33.390 --> 00:01:37.280
Speaking of ears, Chappie’s ears play a
very important part in the movie.

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:41.770
First of all, Chappie’s orange ear allows
us to easily recognise him and distinguish

00:01:41.770 --> 00:01:43.830
him from the other robots in the movie.

00:01:43.830 --> 00:01:48.320
And secondly, Chappie’s ears also gave the
movie’s animators the ability to show us

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:52.760
what Chappie was feeling by placing his ears
in different positions to reveal different

00:01:52.760 --> 00:01:55.320
expressions.

00:01:55.320 --> 00:02:00.510
In addition to visual effects, physical robots
were ALSO used during filming.

00:02:00.510 --> 00:02:06.670
Prop company WETA Workshop built 11 practical
dummies of the movie’s robotic police force,

00:02:06.670 --> 00:02:12.110
and individualised them by swapping out different
number plates and replacing panels with varying

00:02:12.110 --> 00:02:13.180
signs of aging.

00:02:13.180 --> 00:02:18.400
As for the movie’s star robot, there were
three Chappie skeletons in total, and there

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:23.310
were eight sets of panels which could be swapped
out to show each stage of wear and tear that

00:02:23.310 --> 00:02:26.620
Chappie goes through during the film.

00:02:26.620 --> 00:02:31.511
Ninja and Yo-Landi Visser, from the South
African rap-rave group Die Antwoord, star

00:02:31.511 --> 00:02:35.290
in Chappie as former musicians who’ve had
to turn to crime to survive.

00:02:35.290 --> 00:02:40.319
Director Neill Blomkamp encouraged Ninja and
Yo-Landi to bring a lot of their own personalities

00:02:40.319 --> 00:02:41.319
to their roles.

00:02:41.319 --> 00:02:45.430
But he also asked Yo-Landi to show a softer,
more maternal side towards Chappie.

00:02:45.430 --> 00:02:50.849
So, Ninja suggested that Yo-Landi speak to
Chappie in the same cute, high-pitched voice

00:02:50.849 --> 00:02:59.790
she uses talking to her dog.

00:02:59.790 --> 00:03:04.730
Because Chappie features a lot of action,
the stunt team asked the movie’s art department

00:03:04.730 --> 00:03:10.890
to build mini-sets to scale which they used
to help plan complicated action sequences.

00:03:10.890 --> 00:03:14.810
For example, the movie’s opening scene was
a particular challenge as it involved several

00:03:14.810 --> 00:03:20.300
helicopters, gunfire, jumping through windows,
vans getting flipped over and much more!

00:03:20.300 --> 00:03:23.780
Where possible, stunts were done for real,
rather than using visual effects.

00:03:23.780 --> 00:03:28.910
For example, there’s a bike flip where a
droid knocks the rider off a bike which carries

00:03:28.910 --> 00:03:29.910
on spinning.

00:03:29.910 --> 00:03:34.620
To do this, the stunt team put cables on both
the performer and the bike, so the stuntman

00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:37.770
came off the bike while it carried on spinning.

00:03:37.770 --> 00:03:42.790
Although the movie’s set in the South African
city of Johannesburg, a number of the costumes

00:03:42.790 --> 00:03:44.270
have a more American look.

00:03:44.270 --> 00:03:48.670
For instance, Yankie, who’s part of the
gangster trio with Ninja and Yo-Landi, dresses

00:03:48.670 --> 00:03:50.849
in a sort of LA gangster style.

00:03:50.849 --> 00:03:56.150
In contrast to this, gangster boss Hippo’s
look is very South African, very Johannesburg.

00:03:56.150 --> 00:04:01.069
Gabby De Gersigny, who styled all of Ninja
and Yo-Landi’s music videos, also styled

00:04:01.069 --> 00:04:02.400
the rap duo for Chappie.

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:07.379
And according to the movie’s costume designer,
the look of Dev Patel’s character was influenced

00:04:07.379 --> 00:04:11.621
by the old-fashioned conservative styling
of Kempton Park, near Johannesburg, where

00:04:11.621 --> 00:04:14.190
many people work in the defence industry.

00:04:14.190 --> 00:04:18.289
Well there you have it, 8 things you probably
didn't know about Chappie!

00:04:18.289 --> 00:04:21.259
Now, tell me, what’s your favourite robot
movie?

